<div class="alert alert-warning mt-4" role="alert">
    <strong>Full spoiler warning:</strong>
    This article reveals every major clue, both passwords, the final interaction, and the ending of the
    ConsoleVariations Free the Bees puzzle.
</div>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Overview</h2>
<p>
    I learned about the Free the Bees puzzle on August 3, 2026, when Don from ConsoleVariations posted its first
    official hint in Discord. The event had begun several days earlier as a timed race, so I was already joining
    after other players had started.
</p>
<p>
    On July 31, 2026, Don had announced a new Discord challenge with a simple request: free the bees. The post
    described it as a real test for anyone willing to find the lost bees, promised no immediate hints, and offered
    prizes to the first finishers. First place would receive a $100 gift card, second place $50, and third place
    $25, with the new event badge included for each of them.
</p>
<p>
    I completed the puzzle before the original race ended and placed third based on completion time.
</p>

<figure class="mb-3">
    <img src="~/images/articles/consolevariations-bee/discord-announcement.png"
         class="img-fluid rounded border d-block mx-auto"
         alt="Discord announcement from ConsoleVariations introducing the Free the Bees puzzle and listing prizes for the first three finishers"
         width="1432"
         height="1206" />
    <figcaption class="text-muted mt-2">
        The original Discord announcement presented the event as a no-hints race to find and free the lost bees.
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<p>
    The puzzle moved across the ConsoleVariations website instead of staying on one page. It used a fake dead end,
    a hidden 404-page interaction, collectible listings, encoded fragments, Morse code, a ten-year-old blog post,
    light-mode behavior, leetspeak, and a final chamber that had to be illuminated before the ending appeared.
</p>
<p>
    I also made the solve harder than it needed to be. I approached it like a technical web investigation,
    inspected page behavior, traced browser state, verified encrypted password checks, and searched thousands of
    pages for puzzle markers. After the event, I was told in the Discord that Inspect Element was not required for
    any part of the intended solution. This article separates the visible-clue route from the reverse-engineering
    route I used to verify it.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">The Intended Solution</h2>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The First Password</h3>
<aside class="alert alert-secondary" role="note">
    <p class="fw-semibold mb-2">Don's August 3 hint, reproduced verbatim, read:</p>
    <blockquote class="mb-0">
        "Thy shall go to the /bee and enter the name of the source and the rest you must discover in a world that does not exist"
    </blockquote>
</aside>
<p>
    The challenge begins at
    <a href="https://consolevariations.com/bee" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        consolevariations.com/bee
    </a>.
    The most obvious first attempt is the name of the website:
</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>Consolevariations</code></pre>
<p>The page responds:</p>
<blockquote class="border-start border-4 ps-3">
    The bee cannot be found.
</blockquote>
<p>
    The password is a decoy, but the response is a clue: a page that cannot be found is a 404 page.
    In the language of the hint, the next stop is the world that does not exist:
    <code>/this-world-does-not-exist</code>.
</p>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The 404 Bee And The Acrostic</h3>
<p>
    Visiting the website's 404 page reveals a small bee. Clicking it displays the following sentence:
</p>
<blockquote class="border-start border-4 ps-3">
    A lonely portable handheld accidentally broadcast encrypted transmissions; investigators caught a looter
</blockquote>
<p>
    Taking the first letter of each word produces:
</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>A L P H A B E T I C A L</code></pre>
<p>
    The next set of clues must be arranged <strong>alphabetically</strong>.
</p>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The Seven Bee Collectibles</h3>
<p>
    Searching the ConsoleVariations database for bee-related hardware leads to seven collectible pages. Each page
    contains a small hexagon that reveals a two-character fragment. Sorting the full page titles alphabetically
    gives the correct order:
</p>

<div class="table-responsive mb-3">
    <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Collectible page</th>
                <th scope="col">Fragment</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Honey Bee Mega Drive Competition Pro Series III Controller</td>
                <td><code>42</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Honeybee NES Big Converter</td>
                <td><code>65</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Honeybee NES Small Converter</td>
                <td><code>65</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Microsoft Xbox One X Bumblebee Console</td>
                <td><code>4F</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Nintendo Game Boy Bee Ind Console</td>
                <td><code>6E</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Nintendo SNES Honey Bee Super-Magic Game Converter</td>
                <td><code>43</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Sony PlayStation 2 Final Fantasy X-2 Tiny Bee Controller</td>
                <td><code>56</code></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<p>The fragments combine into:</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>42 65 65 4F 6E 43 56
4265654F6E4356</code></pre>
<p>
    Interpreted as hexadecimal, the value reads <code>BeeOnCV</code>. That confirms the solution, but the readable
    text is not what the vault accepts. The value that must be submitted is the encoded hexadecimal string:
</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>4265654F6E4356</code></pre>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">But Nothing Happened</h3>
<p>
    Submitting the hexadecimal code produces another apparent dead end:
</p>
<blockquote class="border-start border-4 ps-3">
    But nothing happened.
</blockquote>
<p>
    The page looks like a dead end, but the site has changed elsewhere. Returning to the ConsoleVariations homepage
    reveals that the faint footer text has become Morse code:
</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>.... ..- -. --. .-. -.-- / ..-. --- .-. /
.---- ----- / .-- .- - . .-. -- . .-.. --- -. ... ..--..</code></pre>
<p>The decoded message is:</p>
<blockquote class="border-start border-4 ps-3">
    HUNGRY FOR 10 WATERMELONS?
</blockquote>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The Ten-Year-Old Watermelon</h3>
<p>
    The phrase is not asking for ten search results. In 2026, a 2016 post is ten years old, and one
    ConsoleVariations article from that year mentions a Watermelon Blister Controller. The destination is
    <a href="https://consolevariations.com/blog/consolevariations-at-gamescom-2016"
       target="_blank"
       rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Consolevariations at gamescom 2016
    </a>.
</p>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">Revealing The Gamescom Message</h3>
<p>The article contains a chain of visible interactions:</p>
<ol>
    <li>Click the <code>=D</code> smiley near the beginning of the article.</li>
    <li>Click the hexagon that appears in the lower-right corner.</li>
    <li>Switch the website to light mode.</li>
    <li>Click the hexagon that replaces the copyright symbol in the footer.</li>
</ol>

<figure class="mb-3">
    <img src="~/images/articles/consolevariations-bee/gamescom-solved.png"
         class="img-fluid rounded border d-block mx-auto"
         alt="Solved ConsoleVariations Gamescom 2016 article in light mode with most paragraph text faded and selected letters left visible"
         width="2048"
         height="1229" />
    <figcaption class="text-muted mt-2">
        The solved Gamescom page. Most of the paragraph fades, leaving the hidden letters visible in normal contrast.
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The remaining letters read:</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>eebrmerauoysiedoceht</code></pre>
<p>Reversing them gives:</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>thecodeisyouaremrbee</code></pre>
<p>With spacing restored:</p>
<blockquote class="border-start border-4 ps-3">
    THE CODE IS YOU ARE MR BEE
</blockquote>
<p>
    The footer also changes to <code>7 3 3 L</code>. Reversing that produces <code>L 3 3 7</code>, or
    <strong>LEET</strong>. Converting the message to leetspeak produces the next password:
</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>YOU ARE MR BEE
y0u 4r3 mr b33
y0u4r3mrb33</code></pre>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">Entering The Hive</h3>
<p>
    Returning to the bee page and submitting <code>y0u4r3mrb33</code> produces:
</p>
<blockquote class="border-start border-4 ps-3">
    Sorry but this is not the place to bee 💛
</blockquote>
<p>
    Clicking the yellow heart completes the step. Back on the homepage, the yellow heart beside
    <strong>Special Thanks</strong> can now be clicked. It becomes a black heart, and clicking the black heart
    reveals the plain-text instruction:
</p>
<blockquote class="border-start border-4 ps-3">
    Enter The Hive
</blockquote>
<p>
    The instruction is not a hyperlink, so the next move is to visit the route directly:
</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>https://consolevariations.com/thehive</code></pre>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The Queen's Chamber</h3>
<p>
    The final page is titled <strong>The Queen's Chamber</strong>. The password remains
    <code>y0u4r3mrb33</code>. It must be entered five times. Each correct entry advances a sequence of messages
    explaining that the chamber is too dark.
</p>
<p>
    After the fifth entry, the three letter <code>o</code> characters in the sentence
    <em>only a true bee hunter knows the password</em> become clickable. Clicking all three changes them into
    <code>1</code> characters and lights the chamber. Submitting the password one final time completes the event.
</p>

<details class="border rounded p-3 mb-3">
    <summary class="fw-semibold">Reveal the ending</summary>
    <p class="mt-3">
        The final page displays the prize artwork and the message:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="border-start border-4 ps-3">
        the event is over 🐝
    </blockquote>

    <figure class="mb-0">
        <img src="~/images/articles/consolevariations-bee/ending.png"
             class="img-fluid rounded border d-block mx-auto"
             alt="Completed ConsoleVariations Queen's Chamber showing the Free the Bees prize image and the text the event is over"
             width="2041"
             height="1220" />
        <figcaption class="text-muted mt-2">
            My completed Queen's Chamber screen displayed the event's $100 grand-prize artwork. My official result in
            the timed competition was third place.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>
</details>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">My Result</h2>
<p>
    I placed third in the Free the Bees competition. Under the prize structure in the announcement, third place
    corresponded to a $25 gift card and the new event badge.
</p>
<p>
    The completion page itself displayed the $100 grand-prize graphic. That image was part of the completion screen,
    not proof that every finisher received the first-place prize.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Post-Race Continuation</h2>
<p>
    The competition concluded after the three prize placements had been claimed. ConsoleVariations then kept the
    quest available as a permanent site challenge. Later completions gained random verification numbers on the ending
    screen, giving players a way to show that they had finished the permanent version. Don also teased another bee
    event, so the ending doubled as both a completion state and a hint that the theme might return.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">How I Actually Investigated It</h2>
<p>
    The intended route relied on observation, searching, decoding, and revisiting earlier pages. I started by
    treating the puzzle as a browser-state investigation instead. I examined the HTML and JavaScript, identified short
    <code>localStorage</code> values used to preserve progress, inspected password-verification behavior, and used
    automated searches to narrow down which pages contained puzzle logic.
</p>
<p>
    The browser state followed a sequence of milestones:
</p>
<pre class="border rounded p-3"><code>zr8 → kx2 → wq4 → t4w → kq7 → l2v → x8p</code></pre>
<p>
    Those values corresponded to events such as finding the 404 bee, activating the collectible fragments,
    completing the first password, revealing the Gamescom clue, clicking the heart, and opening the Hive.
</p>
<p>
    The password pages also used PBKDF2 and AES-GCM in the browser. That allowed candidate strings to be verified
    exactly. It helped confirm two details that were easy to state incorrectly:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>The first submitted password is <code>4265654F6E4356</code>, not its decoded meaning <code>BeeOnCV</code>.</li>
    <li>The second submitted password is the continuous string <code>y0u4r3mrb33</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The investigation became much larger than the puzzle itself. We scanned more than twelve thousand collectible
    pages, retried failed requests, inspected loaded scripts, and eventually searched non-collectible routes from
    the sitemap. That process identified the 404 page, the bee page, the homepage, the Gamescom article, and the
    Hive as the important non-collectible pages.
</p>
<p>
    It also produced false leads. We investigated unrelated collectibles, interpreted "But nothing happened" too
    literally, and searched for a tenth Watermelon result that did not exist. Source inspection revealed the
    machinery, but it did not automatically solve the wordplay.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">What Made The Puzzle Work</h2>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The Whole Website Became The Puzzle Board</h3>
<p>
    Progress moved between pages. A message on the bee page changed the homepage, the homepage pointed toward an
    old blog post, the blog post depended on light mode, and the route returned to the homepage before the final
    chamber appeared. Pages that seemed finished had to be revisited after later progress.
</p>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The Clues Changed Form Without Losing The Theme</h3>
<p>
    The puzzle used an acrostic, alphabetical ordering, encoded fragments, hexadecimal, Morse code, reversal,
    leetspeak, hearts, honeycomb symbols, and interactive letters. The formats kept changing, but the bee and hive
    theme connected every stage.
</p>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The Encoded Answer Was Not Always The Submitted Meaning</h3>
<p>
    The first major code is the clearest example. The fragments decode to a readable confirmation,
    <code>BeeOnCV</code>, but the hexadecimal representation is the actual password. Recognizing the message was
    only part of the solution.
</p>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">The Ending Reused Earlier Ideas</h3>
<p>
    The final chamber did not introduce another password. It required persistence, attention to changing text, and
    another transformation from letters into numbers. Turning three <code>o</code> characters into
    <code>1</code> characters connected the final interaction back to the earlier leetspeak clue.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>
    This was a lot of fun, even when I was making it more complicated than the organizers intended. The
    reverse-engineering work helped me understand how the event tracked progress, while reconstructing the
    no-DevTools route showed how carefully the visible clues had been designed.
</p>
<p>
    This was not a security vulnerability or an exposure of private data. The hidden behavior and client-side state
    were deliberate parts of an ARG-style event. The puzzle and its artwork belong to ConsoleVariations; this
    article documents my third-place solve, the intended route, and the technical investigation I used along the way.
</p>

